# gate/treesitter/agreement.baseline — the exact disagreement between the two
# grammar authorities, pinned so that a TENTH cannot arrive quietly.
#
# Produced by `idol run gate/treesitter/agreement.id`, which reads the owner's
# generated bridge (identity, spelling, infix face, precedence, associativity —
# all on one row) and ext/tree-sitter-idol/grammar.js. Every line below is a
# place where the authored editor grammar and lib/compiler/token.id say
# different things about the SAME operator.
#
# A RATCHET, NOT A BUDGET. The gate fails when a divergence appears that is not
# here, AND when a line here no longer describes a divergence. Seven pins were
# deleted the first time this ran against a newer editor grammar: `..` and `~`
# had been moved into the owner's band upstream, and the gate said so by name
# rather than passing quietly on a stale count.

# ── 7 comparisons: the owner says NONASSOC, tree-sitter says LEFT.
# `a < b < c` is refused by the compiler and accepted by the editor grammar.
# tree-sitter can express this — a plain `prec(n, …)` leaves the conflict
# unresolved instead of silently choosing a side.
assoc in nonassoc left
assoc < nonassoc left
assoc > nonassoc left
assoc == nonassoc left
assoc != nonassoc left
assoc <= nonassoc left
assoc >= nonassoc left

# ── infix `@` (matmul) exists for the compiler and not for the editor.
missing @

# ── `~=` is recognized by the editor grammar as an operator the owner does not
# own. src/grammar_roles.zig records that this spelling moved to xor-assign,
# and lib/compiler/lexer.id refuses to glue it: "`~` is XOR and nothing else".
extra ~=
